On 2008-12-17, Marc Hartstein <marcmagus@gmail.com> wrote:
> On 2008-12-17, Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com> wrote:
>> Wouldn't a stricter restriction of artifact wishing or a prohibition
>> of wishing for quest artifacts of other classes be something that
>> would fit SporkHacks design concept?
> The idea, as I understand it, is more to make it so there is no single
> obvious choice (EoA if Neu) for your artifact wish(es) if you contrive
> to get one/more.  That should help differentiate those based on role
> preferences and play style.

This is pretty much it.  Direct veto of wishing for quest artifacts
would simply change what was the 'best' wish without significantly
affecting things.

> I believe he *has* decreased the number of wishes generally available,
> as well.

Randomly generated wands of wishing are now always generated (1:X), and
the probability of magic lamps in shops is somewhat smaller.

The goal is twofold here; increase the range of appealing wishes, such
that players will be more inclined to wish for things based on their
overall situation rather than the objective "best" (and then contrive
their situation to match that "best"), and also reduce the number of
wishes in general so that players will be forced to make do in at least
part with what they find on the ground.

